<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsudaira Tadanao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1595">1595</a>–<a href="/years/1650">1650</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsudaira Tadanao was the second lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> domain in Echizen, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuki-hideyasu">Yuki Hideyasu</a>. He fought at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1615 but became resentful of his treatment by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>. In 1623 he was removed for misconduct and exiled to Bungo, and the Fukui domain was reduced.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsudaira-tadanao.jpg" alt="Matsudaira Tadanao" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 伝忠直筆 / Allegedly a self-portrait · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matsudaira_Tadanao_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tadanao was born in Settsu Province on 16 July 1595, the eldest son of Yūki Hideyasu; his childhood name was Senchiyomaru. In 1603 he travelled to Edo with his father and was received by his uncle, Shōgun Tokugawa Hidetada, who took a great liking to him. He was given the courtesy title Mikawa-no-kami and court rank in 1605. On his father's death in 1607 he became daimyō of the 670,000 koku Fukui Domain, and in 1611 he married Katsuhime, a daughter of Hidetada, taking a character of Hidetada's name as Matsudaira Tadanao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His rule began badly. In 1612 and 1613 armed conflict broke out among his senior retainers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> settled it only with difficulty, blaming Tadanao's youth and inexperience. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> troops in the Osaka Winter Campaign but lost control of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and was severely scolded by his grandfather, the retired shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. In the Summer Campaign that followed he took the head of Sanada Yukimura and led his men at the very tip of the Tokugawa advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>.</p><p>He received no reward for this. His court rank stayed at senior third rank and councillor, below the Middle Councillor his father had held, though he was given further courtesy titles in 1615. Taking this as an insult, he feigned illness in 1621 and skipped his required sankin kōtai journey to Edo. He was also said to have tried to poison his wife, who was saved when one of her maids took her place, and to have led his soldiers on rampages through his retainers' homes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Hidetada</a> banished him to Funai Domain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bungo-province">Bungo Province</a> with an allowance of 10,000 koku. He became a Buddhist priest under the name Ippaku and lived at first at Ogiwara village on the coast, until local authorities, fearing he might escape, made him move inland. He died on 5 October 1650, aged 56. His son Matsudaira Mitsunaga was moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> to Takada Domain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echigo-province">Echigo Province</a>, while his brother Tadamasa received Fukui, which the family then held until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matsudaira Tadanao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hideyasu's son <b>Matsudaira Tadanao</b> was exiled in 1623 for misconduct, and the domain was cut in stages, falling to 250,000 koku in 1686 before being partly restored to 320,000.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukui-domain">Fukui Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Settsu Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1630" aria-label="Edo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8080 875 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matsudaira Tadanao"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8530 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8530 1145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1630">What was here in 1630? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
